  "notes": "{揺|ゆ}する is a transitive verb ({他動詞|たどうし}). The intransitive pair is {揺|ゆ}れる (to shake, to sway).\n\nASPECT ({ている}):\n{揺|ゆ}すっている indicates an ongoing action of shaking something.\n\nCOMMON PATTERNS:\n- {肩|かた}を{揺|ゆ}する - to shake someone's shoulder\n- {木|き}を{揺|ゆ}する - to shake a tree\n- {揺|ゆ}り{起|お}こす - to shake someone awake\n- {揺|ゆ}すり{落|お}とす - to shake something off\n\n{揺|ゆ}する VS {揺|ゆ}れる:\n- {揺|ゆ}する (transitive): someone shakes something\n- {揺|ゆ}れる (intransitive): something shakes/sways on its own\n\nNOTE ON SENSE 2:\nThe slang meaning \"to extort\" comes from the image of shaking someone down for money. This usage is informal.",
